<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Third Dominican Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1924 CE – 1965 CE · capital Santo Domingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Dominican state from 1924 to 1965, dominated for three decades by the brutal dictatorship of Rafael Trujillo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/military-government-of-santo-domingo">Military Government of Santo Domingo</a> ended in 1924 with the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horacio-vasquez">Horacio Vásquez</a>. His attempt to extend his term weakened his government, and in 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Rafael Trujillo</a>, commander of the US-trained army, took power in a coup and won a fraudulent election.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Trujillo</a> ruled directly or through puppet presidents until 1961, controlling the economy through family monopolies, including much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> industry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> was renamed Ciudad Trujillo in 1936. In October 1937 his troops massacred thousands of Haitians along the border, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsley-massacre">Parsley Massacre</a>. He paid off the foreign debt and built roads and ports, while the secret police tortured and murdered opponents, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirabal-sisters">Mirabal sisters</a> in 1960.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Trujillo</a> was assassinated on 30 May 1961. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bosch">Juan Bosch</a> won the 1962 election but was overthrown in 1963. In April 1965 a civil war between constitutionalists and the military led to US intervention, and elections in 1966 began the present <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/third-dominican-republic.jpg" alt="Third Dominican Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Juan Pablo Duarte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dominican_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1924 CE – 1965 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santo Domingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Horacio Vásquez, Rafael Trujillo, Juan Bosch</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dominican Republic (1924-1965), Trujillo era</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=third-dominican-republic" aria-label="Third Dominican Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Third Dominican Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3105 1749L3105 1749M3105 1749L3105 1749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3105 1749h0M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3105 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santo Domingo. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=third-dominican-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
